{"cuisine/cuisine-of-montserrat":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cuisine-of-montserrat","n":"Montserrat cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Montserrat","intro":[["Montserrat's national dish is goat water, a (not too thick or thin) goat meat stew (made from the meat of the male (ram) goat) and served with crispy bread rolls. It bears resemblance to the Irish stew and can be served with a variety of foods, such as bread, and rice."],["The Montserrat cuisine resembles the general British and Caribbean one, as it is situated in the Caribbean zone and it is a British territory. The cuisine includes a wide range of light meats, like fish, seafood and chicken, which are mostly grilled, fried or roasted. It is a fusion of multiple cultures, including Spanish, French, African, Indian and Amerindian."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cuisine-of-montserrat"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuisine_of_Montserrat"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"cuisine/balkan-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/balkan-cuisine","n":"Balkan cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"Cuisine of a people","intro":[["Balkan cuisine encompasses a collection of national cuisines that combine characteristics of European cuisine with some of those from West Asia. In its broadest sense, Balkan cuisine encompasses the native cuisines of Albania, Bosnia and Herzegovina, Bulgaria, Croatia, Greece, Hungary, Moldova, Montenegro, North Macedonia, Romania, Serbia and Slovenia. Except for Slovenian cuisine, these cuisines have a notable Turkish influence due to Ottoman occupation. Except for Hungarian, Moldovan, and Romanian cuisine, Balkan cuisines have considerable Mediterranean influence, mostly present in Greek and Albanian cuisine. Romani cuisine, the traditional food of the Romani people, includes dishes from traditional Balkan cuisine."],["Balkan cuisine is available in Vienna, Austria, due to post-WWII migration to the city. Germany has restaurants serving Balkan cuisine, which were often called Yugoslavian restaurants until the outbreak of the Yugoslav Wars. Balkan Grills have been present in West Germany since the 1960s, popularizing ćevapčići in the country, but these establishments have become rarer since the late 1980s, and those that survive are often now called \"Croatian\" instead."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"balkan-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Balkan_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"The Balkan Kitchen: Recipes and Stories from the Heart of the Balkans","ref":"cookbook/the-balkan-kitchen-recipes-and-stories-from-the-heart-of-the-balkans","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Irina Janakievska","note":"Macedonian-born author's recipes from across the Balkan region, published by Hardie Grant.","url":"https://publishing.hardiegrant.com/en-gb/books/the-balkan-kitchen-by-irina-janakievska/9781784886851","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":5,"links":[["Ajvar","dish/ajvar"],["Ayran","dish/ayran"],["Stuffed tomatoes","dish/stuffed-tomatoes"],["Tarator","dish/tarator"],["Torshi","dish/torshi"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Yogurt","ingredient/yogurt"],["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Cucumber","ingredient/cucumber"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Juglans regia","ingredient/juglans-regia"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Qatiq","ingredient/qatiq"],["Strained yogurt","ingredient/strained-yogurt"],["Walnut","ingredient/walnut"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Vinegar","ingredient/vinegar"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Within Balkan cooking","count":9,"links":[["Albanian cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-albania"],["Aromanian cuisine","cuisine/aromanian-cuisine"],["Bosnian cuisine","cuisine/bosnian-cuisine"],["Bulgarian cuisine","cuisine/bulgarian-cuisine"],["Croatian cuisine","cuisine/croatian-cuisine"],["Gagauz cuisine","cuisine/gagauz-cuisine"],["Macedonian cuisine","cuisine/macedonian-cuisine"],["Montenegrin cuisine","cuisine/montenegrin-cuisine"],["Serbian cuisine","cuisine/serbian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/f/f1/Roastlamb.JPG/500px-Roastlamb.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"No machine-readable author provided. Preslav~commonswiki assumed (based on copyright…","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roastlamb.JPG"}},"ingredient/cubanelle":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/cubanelle","n":"Cubanelle","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 3 cuisines","intro":[["The cubanelle, also known as the \"Cuban pepper\" (ají cubanela), is a varietal of mild sweet pepper from the species Capsicum annuum. When unripe, it is a light, yellowish-green color, but will turn bright red if allowed to ripen. Compared to bell peppers, it has thinner flesh, is more elongated, and has a slightly more \"wrinkled\" appearance."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-cubanelle"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cubanelle"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Andorran cuisine","cuisine/andorran-cuisine",[["Arròs negre","dish/arros-negre"]]],["Catalan cuisine","cuisine/catalan-cuisine",[["Arròs negre","dish/arros-negre"]]],["Valencian cuisine","cuisine/valencian-cuisine",[["Arròs negre","dish/arros-negre"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Cephalopod ink","ingredient/cephalopod-ink"],["Cuttlefish","ingredient/cuttlefish"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Squid as food","ingredient/squid-as-food"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/26/Cubanelle_Peppers.jpg/500px-Cubanelle_Peppers.jpg","w":500,"h":376,"by":"Unknown author","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cubanelle_Peppers.jpg"}},"ingredient/shrimp-paste":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/shrimp-paste","n":"Shrimp paste","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 10 dishes across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Shrimp paste, shrimp sauce, or prawn sauce is a fermented condiment commonly used in Southeast Asian and coastal Chinese cuisines. It is primarily made from finely crushed shrimp or krill mixed with salt, and then fermented for several weeks. Depending on the regional variations, it is sold either in its wet form or sun-dried, and either cut into blocks or sold in bulk. It is an essential ingredient in many curries, sauces and sambal. Shrimp paste can be found in many meals in Cambodia, Indonesia, Laos, Malaysia, Myanmar, the Philippines, Singapore, Thailand, and Vietnam. It is often an ingredient in dip for fish or vegetables."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-shrimp-paste"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shrimp_paste"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Ayam goreng kalasan","dish/ayam-goreng-kalasan"],["Ayam Taliwang","dish/ayam-taliwang"],["Kapurung","dish/kapurung"],["Karedok","dish/karedok"],["Nasi gandul","dish/nasi-gandul"],["Nasi jamblang","dish/nasi-jamblang"],["Plecing kangkung","dish/plecing-kangkung"],["Sambal","dish/sambal"],["Sambal tuktuk","dish/sambal-tuktuk"],["Udang balado","dish/udang-balado"]]],["Balinese cuisine","cuisine/balinese-cuisine",[["Plecing kangkung","dish/plecing-kangkung"]]],["Javanese cuisine","cuisine/javanese-cuisine",[["Nasi gandul","dish/nasi-gandul"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Candlenut","ingredient/candlenut"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Palm sugar","ingredient/palm-sugar"],["Sweet soy sauce","ingredient/sweet-soy-sauce"],["Anchovy","ingredient/anchovy"],["Kencur","ingredient/kencur"],["Mung bean sprout","ingredient/mung-bean-sprout"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Sambal","ingredient/sambal"],["Anchovies as food","ingredient/anchovies-as-food"],["Chilli pepper","ingredient/chilli-pepper"],["Shallot","ingredient/shallot"],["Ayam goreng","ingredient/ayam-goreng"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/37/A_square_of_shrimp_paste_from_Hung_Kee_store.jpg/500px-A_square_of_shrimp_paste_from_Hung_Kee_store.jpg","w":500,"h":282,"by":"Peachyeung316","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_square_of_shrimp_paste_from_Hung_Kee_store.jpg"}},"ingredient/buttermilk":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/buttermilk","n":"Buttermilk","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 4 dishes across 4 cuisines","intro":[["Buttermilk is a dairy drink made by adding lactic acid bacteria to milk to produce a fermented dairy drink. Traditionally, it was made from the nearly fat-free milk remaining after churning butter from cream, which was cultured with natural bacteria prior to and during churning, giving a slight sour taste to the buttermilk and preventing spoilage. However, with the ubiquity of refrigeration in industrialized countries, butter in those areas is typically made from uncultured or \"sweet\" cream. Therefore, most modern buttermilk is specifically produced by inoculating fresh, pasteurized milk, and is available in different levels of fat content."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-buttermilk"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buttermilk"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":4,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Ranch dressing","dish/ranch-dressing"]]],["Danish cuisine","cuisine/danish-cuisine",[["Æbleskiver","dish/bleskiver"]]],["Iranian cuisine","cuisine/iranian-cuisine",[["Doogh","dish/doogh"]]],["Southern American cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-southern-united-states",[["Chess pie","dish/chess-pie"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Corn meal","ingredient/corn-meal"],["Granulated sugar","ingredient/granulated-sugar"],["Shortcrust pastry","ingredient/shortcrust-pastry"],["Vanilla","ingredient/vanilla"],["Yogurt","ingredient/yogurt"],["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Chives","ingredient/chives"],["Dill","ingredient/dill"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Mayonnaise","ingredient/mayonnaise"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Parsley","ingredient/parsley"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/23/Buttermilk-%28right%29-and-Milk-%28left%29.jpg/500px-Buttermilk-%28right%29-and-Milk-%28left%29.jpg","w":500,"h":335,"by":"Ukko-wc","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buttermilk-(right)-and-Milk-(left).jpg"}},"ingredient/conch":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/conch","n":"Conch","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Conch is any of a number of different medium-to-large-sized sea snails. Conch shells typically have a high spire and a noticeable siphonal canal (in other words, the shell comes to a noticeable point on both ends)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-conch"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conch"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Bahamian cuisine","cuisine/bahamian-cuisine",[["Conch fritter","dish/conch-fritter"]]]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/4e/Queen_Conch_%28Lobatus_gigas%29.jpg/500px-Queen_Conch_%28Lobatus_gigas%29.jpg","w":500,"h":400,"by":"Daniel Neal from Sacramento, CA, US","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Queen_Conch_(Lobatus_gigas).jpg"}},"dish/delta-cafe":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/delta-cafe","n":"Delta Cafe","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Delta Cafe is a Southern, cajun, and soul food restaurant in the Woodstock neighborhood of Portland, Oregon, United States. Anastasia Corya and Anton Pace opened the restaurant in 1995. They sold Delta in 2007 to open another Southern restaurant, Miss Delta, with two of the cafe's cooks. Frequented by Reed College students and neighborhood residents, Delta Cafe has been recognized as a favorite local comfort food destination. The cafe also has a cocktail bar called Delta Lounge. In 2016, a mural was painted on the restaurant's exterior as part of a neighborhood beautification project."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-delta-cafe"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delta_Cafe"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Cajun cuisine","cuisine/cajun-cuisine"],["Soul food","cuisine/soul-food"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Kenneth Temple: Cajun and Creole","ref":"site/kennethtemple-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"kennethtemple.com","note":"Cajun and Creole recipes from a New Orleans-based cook and video creator.","url":"https://kennethtemple.com/recipes/cajun-creole/","suggested":true},{"name":"The Soul Food Pot","ref":"site/thesoulfoodpot-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"thesoulfoodpot.com","note":"Shaunda Necole's blog of soul food and Southern recipes.","url":"https://thesoulfoodpot.com/recipe-index","suggested":true},{"name":"Cooks with Soul","ref":"site/cookswithsoul-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"cookswithsoul.com","note":"Soul food recipe site that also covers cookbooks by Black authors.","url":"https://cookswithsoul.com/2024-holiday-gift-guide-soul-food-cookbooks-by-black-authors/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Cajun cuisine","links":[["Acadia: A New Orleans Bistro","dish/acadia-a-new-orleans-bistro"],["Blue Bayou Restaurant","dish/blue-bayou-restaurant"],["Gumbo","dish/gumbo"],["Le Bistro Montage","dish/le-bistro-montage"],["New Orleans hot sausage","dish/new-orleans-hot-sausage"],["The Parish","dish/the-parish"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/a/a9/Delta_Cafe_in_Woodstock%2C_Portland%2C_Oregon_2012.JPG/500px-Delta_Cafe_in_Woodstock%2C_Portland%2C_Oregon_2012.JPG","w":500,"h":667,"by":"Another Believer","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Delta_Cafe_in_Woodstock%2C_Portland%2C_Oregon_2012.JPG"}},"ingredient/pot-roast":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/pot-roast","n":"Pot roast","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Pot roast is a beef dish made by slow-cooking a (usually tough) cut of beef in moist heat, on a kitchen stove top with a covered vessel or pressure cooker, or in an oven or slow cooker."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-pot-roast"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pot_roast"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Minnesotan cuisine","cuisine/minnesotan-cuisine",[["Hot beef commercial","dish/hot-beef-commercial"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Gravy","ingredient/gravy"],["Mashed potato","ingredient/mashed-potato"],["White bread","ingredient/white-bread"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/b/be/American_pot_roast.jpg/500px-American_pot_roast.jpg","w":500,"h":380,"by":"Mark Miller","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:American_pot_roast.jpg"}},"dish/imko-s":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/imko-s","n":"Imko's","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Restaurant Imko's is a defunct restaurant in IJmuiden, Netherlands. It was a fine dining restaurant that was awarded one Michelin star in 1999 and retained that rating until 2003. Due to deterioration of the port area where the restaurant started suffering. Owner Imko Binnerts tried to sell the restaurant in 2003 but failed, seeing the restaurant go bankrupt in 2004. The building itself was demolished in 2007."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-imko-s"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imko's"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine"],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Pardon Your French","ref":"site/pardonyourfrench-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"pardonyourfrench.com","note":"Audrey Le Goff, a French cook, shares classic and regional French home recipes.","url":"https://pardonyourfrench.com","suggested":true},{"name":"La Cuisine de Géraldine","ref":"site/lacuisinedegeraldine-fr","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"lacuisinedegeraldine.fr","note":"French home cooking blog by Géraldine Leverd, also author of The New French Kitchen.","url":"https://lacuisinedegeraldine.fr/en/about-us","suggested":true},{"name":"New Caledonia Tourism: Kanak cooking","ref":"site/newcaledonia-travel","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"newcaledonia.travel","note":"Official tourism board overview of Kanak food, including bougna cooked in an earth oven.","url":"https://www.newcaledonia.travel/nz/gastronomy-wine/gastronomy/kanak-cooking","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from French cuisine","links":[["'t Fornuis","dish/t-fornuis"],["'t Misverstant","dish/t-misverstant"],["'t Vlasbloemeken","dish/t-vlasbloemeken"],["&moshik","dish/moshik"],["Aan de Poel","dish/aan-de-poel"],["Adrian (restaurant)","dish/adrian-restaurant"],["Adventureland Bazaar","dish/adventureland-bazaar"],["Alain Ducasse at The Dorchester","dish/alain-ducasse-at-the-dorchester"],["Albufera sauce","dish/albufera-sauce"],["Alex (restaurant)","dish/alex-restaurant"],["Andouillette","dish/andouillette"],["Arpège (restaurant)","dish/arpege-restaurant"],["Aspic","dish/aspic"],["Atelier Crenn","dish/atelier-crenn"],["Au Coin des Bons Enfants","dish/au-coin-des-bons-enfants"],["Au Crocodile","dish/au-crocodile"],["Au Pied de Cochon (Mexico City)","dish/au-pied-de-cochon-mexico-city"],["Auberge (restaurant)","dish/auberge-restaurant"],["Auberge De Kieviet","dish/auberge-de-kieviet"],["Aubergine (London restaurant)","dish/aubergine-london-restaurant"],["Auguste (restaurant)","dish/auguste-restaurant"],["Bagatelle (restaurant)","dish/bagatelle-restaurant"],["Balthazar (restaurant)","dish/balthazar-restaurant"],["Bardot Brasserie","dish/bardot-brasserie"],["Bâtard (restaurant)","dish/batard-restaurant"],["Baumé (restaurant)","dish/baume-restaurant"],["Bayonne ham","dish/bayonne-ham"],["Be Our Guest Restaurant","dish/be-our-guest-restaurant"],["Beaulieu, Doorwerth Castle","dish/beaulieu-doorwerth-castle"],["Beef Stroganoff","dish/beef-stroganoff"],["Bell's (restaurant)","dish/bell-s-restaurant"],["Belmond Le Manoir aux Quat'Saisons","dish/belmond-le-manoir-aux-quat-saisons"],["Beluga (restaurant)","dish/beluga-restaurant"],["Berowra Waters Inn","dish/berowra-waters-inn"],["Beurre maître d'hôtel","dish/beurre-maitre-d-hotel"],["Beurre manié","dish/beurre-manie"],["Bisque (food)","dish/bisque-food"],["Bistro 990","dish/bistro-990"],["Bistro Agnes","dish/bistro-agnes"],["Bistro Jeanty","dish/bistro-jeanty"]]}]},"ingredient/au-jus":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/au-jus","n":"Au jus","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Au jus is a French culinary term meaning \"with juice\". It refers to meat dishes prepared or served together with a light broth or gravy, made from the fluids secreted by the meat as it is cooked. In French cuisine, cooking au jus is a natural way to enhance the flavour of dishes, mainly chicken, veal, and lamb. In American cuisine, the term is sometimes used to refer to a light sauce for beef recipes, which may be served with the food or placed on the side for dipping."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-au-jus"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Au_jus"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Italian-American cuisine","cuisine/italian-american-cuisine",[["Italian beef","dish/italian-beef"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Baguette","ingredient/baguette"],["Bread roll","ingredient/bread-roll"],["Giardiniera","ingredient/giardiniera"],["Roast beef","ingredient/roast-beef"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/4d/French_dip.jpg/500px-French_dip.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Nick Ares","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:French_dip.jpg"}},"ingredient/lurou":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/lurou","n":"Lurou","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Lu rou is a regional Chinese meat dish made via braising with master stock, often made with pork. In Southern Taiwan, the dish is called bah-sò (肉燥), its Hokkien name. Notable variations of this dish include Teochew lo mei, Thai khao kha mu, Taiwanese Ló͘-bah-pn̄g, and Fujianese braised pork rice."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-lurou"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lu_rou"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Taiwan cuisine","cuisine/taiwan-cuisine",[["Ló͘-bah-pn̄g","dish/lo-bah-png"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Rice wine","ingredient/rice-wine"],["Soy sauce","ingredient/soy-sauce"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/f/f8/HK_Wan_Chai_%E6%98%A5%E5%9C%92%E8%A1%97_Spring_Garden_Lane_night_Chiu_Chow_food_shop_window.jpg/500px-HK_Wan_Chai_%E6%98%A5%E5%9C%92%E8%A1%97_Spring_Garden_Lane_night_Chiu_Chow_food_shop_window.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Springat","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HK_Wan_Chai_%E6%98%A5%E5%9C%92%E8%A1%97_Spring_Garden_Lane_night_Chiu_Chow_food_shop_window.jpg"}},"dish/nasi-ambeng":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/nasi-ambeng","n":"Nasi ambeng","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Javanese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Nasi ambeng is an Indonesian (Javanese) fragrant rice dish that consists of—but is not limited to—steamed white rice, chicken curry or chicken stewed in soy sauce, beef or chicken rendang, sambal goreng (lit. fried sambal; a mildly spicy stir-fried relish commonly made with firm tofu, tempeh, and long beans) urap, perkedel, and serundeng."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-nasi-ambeng"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nasi_ambeng"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Javanese cuisine","cuisine/javanese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":6,"links":[["Perkedel","ingredient/perkedel"],["Rempeyek","ingredient/rempeyek"],["Rendang","ingredient/rendang"],["Sambal","ingredient/sambal"],["Tempeh","ingredient/tempeh"],["Urap","ingredient/urap"]]},{"h":"More from Javanese cuisine","links":[["Buntil","dish/buntil"],["Getuk goreng","dish/getuk-goreng"],["Javanese doughnut","dish/javanese-doughnut"],["Megono","dish/megono"],["Nasi gandul","dish/nasi-gandul"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/26/Nasi_ambeng.jpg/500px-Nasi_ambeng.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Li Tsin Soon from